  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- $B$ is invertible",
  "worked_solution": "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
